  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nAnomalous ridging across the Upper Great Lakes into Ontario and\nQuebec remains in place this week. With the ridge sitting atop \na broad area of high pressure across the TN Valley, ensemble low\ntracks are steered away from the Northeast and northern New \nEngland through at least this coming weekend.\n\nResulting conditions at the surface will be decidedly dry with\nabove normal temperatures (particularly daytime highs).\nLocations from southern NH through the ME/NH Lakes Region, and\nup most of the southern Maine coast could see highs around 60 to\nthe lower 60s. The exception would be still and cloud-free\nnights resulting in cooler radiational cooling opportunities.\nThis dry stretch will continue to result in growing rainfall\ndeficits, worsening the ongoing drought that is impacting much\nof the region.\n\nA glance past this weekend, the eventual retreat of high \npressure could feature a window of storminess. While multiple \nmodels suggest this possibility, they also differ in time and \nlocation. This looks to be the phasing, or lack thereof, of a \ncoastal low moving up the Eastern Seaboard with approaching \ntrough from the west. Will monitor this period for the next\nchance of notable precipitation in the region, but can also see\nsome solutions where precip remains light.",
